<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- Exactly one entitlement, and it arrives with the feature that needs it.

     `com.apple.developer.applesignin` is what lets the app present an
     `ASAuthorizationAppleIDRequest`. It is claimed now because this slice ships
     the real system Sign in with Apple button and the hardened server exchange
     behind it; it was absent through the previous three slices for the same
     reason it is present now — an entitlement is a claim to the OS and to the
     user, and each one lands with the functional slice that earns it.

     `Default` (rather than `Sign In With Apple`) is the plain capability: this
     app is not the primary of an app group that shares an Apple ID relationship
     with another app.

     A SIGNED build additionally needs the com.relayium.app App ID to carry the
     Sign in with Apple capability in its provisioning profile, and the server's
     RELAYIUM_APPLE_CLIENT_IDS to include com.relayium.app. Neither is something
     this file can establish, and neither is proved by a simulator build.

     The bearer token and the stored-link keys still live in this app's OWN
     default per-app keychain access group — the one every app gets — which is
     why no `keychain-access-groups` entitlement appears here. Naming a group
     would claim a credential share with another app that does not exist, and on
     a signed device build it would fail with `errSecMissingEntitlement`
     (-34018).

     `PhotosPicker` likewise needs neither an entitlement nor an
     `NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription`: it runs out of process and hands back only
     the items the user picked, so this app never has library access to ask for.

     Still absent, each with the slice that would earn it: Associated Domains
     (link handoff), an App Group and a shared keychain group (a Share
     Extension), background modes (transfers surviving suspension), push, IAP.

     Local network is NOT on that list, and R3-E's version of this comment was
     wrong to put it there. Relayium's nearby feature is not Bonjour and does
     not scan: it joins the service's code-less rendezvous room over the same
     origin as everything else, and the server groups that room by the public IP
     it observes. R3-F therefore shipped the whole Nearby tab — roster, explicit
     send and unsolicited receive — without adding any entitlement, any
     NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription, any NSBonjourServices or the multicast
     entitlement. Asking for local-network access would be a permission prompt
     for something this app does not do. -->
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
